  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 145 AM Friday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Today may be the last 80 degree day for the season \nat many locations, with well above normal temperatures forecast.\n\nSome patchy fog may occur again this morning, with the I-95 corridor \nthe favored location today. However, the low clouds and fog should \nclear by late morning, with just a few passing high clouds this \nafternoon. While typical conversations revolve around the first and \nlast 90 degree days of the year, today may be a day to talk about \nthe last 80 degree day of the year. This typically occurs on October \n21 at Greensboro, October 31 at RDU, and November 11 at \nFayetteville. Have added a climate section below in the discussion \nas this forecast package increased the forecast highs at all three \nclimate sites, although record highs are not forecast to be reached \n(values ranging from 84 to 87). A dry cold front will push south \ninto North Carolina tonight, dropping high temperatures to a range \nfrom the mid 60s to the mid 70s on Saturday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Widespread cloud cover and mostly virga expected \nlate Sat night into Mon. Good chance for measurable rain Wed into \nFri, but timing and amounts remain highly uncertain.\n\nConfidence continues to increase in a dry, no measurable rain, \nforecast for this weekend into Mon as most model guidance trends \ntowards mostly increasing cloud cover and at best non-measurable \nvery light rain falling from mid-lvl cloud bases. Moisture loosely \nassociated with remnants of TC Vance is still expected to lift into \nthe central Plains tonight into Sat before slowly spreading out \nacross the eastern CONUS into early next week. However, most of this \nmoisture will likely remain in the mid/upper-lvls once reaching the \nMid-Atlantic and Carolinas as a reinforcing surge of cooler and \ndrier air will precede the influx of moisture. Lift remains very \nlimited and mostly confined to a sharpening upper trough and any mid-\nlvl disturbances within the shearing out wave. The deterministic GFS \nappears to be the final hold out and still brings minor \naccumulations within a stronger mid-lvl wave, but this solution is \nan outlier at this point. Near to slightly above normal PWAT values, \nagain confined mostly to the mid/upper levels, will likely still \ncombine with the forcing from the upper trough to support increasing \ncloud cover and periods of virga, but this chance for measurable \nrain appears to have dried up. \n\nThe better signal for measurable rainfall continues to be sometime \nwithin the Wed into Fri night timeframe as a strong mid/upper low \nshifts from the West Coast on Sun into the Central Plains and MS \nValley into the mid-week timeframe. How this feature will develop \nand evolve is understandably uncertain as the driving features will \nnot phase until this weekend with potentially additional smaller \nscale waves influencing its evolution into the late week period. \nLooking closer from a global and AI ensemble guidance hardly paints \na picture for significant rain. The wettest ensemble solution is the \nAIGEFS and its 50th percentile 24 hour QPF ranges from 0.25 to 0.5\" \nacross the Carolinas, greatest in the mountains. Whether the warm \nfront is able to lift into or even through our area (to increase \ninstability and rain rates) is highly uncertain and further \ncomplicated by a trend within the AIFS ensemble, which is \nhighlighting a surface low development closer to the Carolina coast, \nwhich would persist northerly winds across the area and keep central \nNC within stratiform precipitation and greatly limiting the higher-\nend rainfall potential.",
